    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:macro-b1819-cattle-illness-observation","source_ref":"source:macro-b1819-msu-sick-cattle","anchor":{"section":"Visual Signs of Cattle Illness; Identifying Injured Cattle"},"note":"MSU lists reduced appetite, gaunt appearance, laboured breathing, cough, eye/nasal discharge, altered manure, depression, lagging and reluctance to rise or move as observations that can indicate illness or injury in cattle, and directs veterinary advice for situations outside a standard plan. These observations are non-specific; no diagnosis, cause, treatment, animal, food, herd, or local helper follows.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:macro-b1819-cow-calf-separation-stress","source_ref":"source:macro-b1819-uw-weaning-stress","anchor":{"section":"Choose a Weaning Method to Decrease Stress on Calves; Weaning Methods"},"note":"UW Extension identifies maternal bonds and describes abrupt total separation as more stressful than approaches retaining sensory contact; it also notes familiar food and water sources at separation. This establishes a qualitative transition stress relation only, not a particular cow, calf, age, fence, pasture, ration, duration, handling method, or outcome.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:macro-b1819-sheep-wet-cold-shelter","source_ref":"source:macro-b1819-aces-wet-cold-ruminants","anchor":{"section":"What is cold stress in wet weather?; Management Strategies to Reduce Risk"},"note":"Alabama Extension explains that rain, snow, mud or damp bedding reduce coat insulation and increase heat loss; shelter, wind protection, drainage and dry bedding limit exposure. This is general small-ruminant biology and care context, not a local weather claim, animal condition, shelter presence, diagnosis, exact temperature, ration, or outcome.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:macro-b1819-smoke-eye-irritation","source_ref":"source:macro-b1819-cdc-wildfire-smoke","anchor":{"section":"Safety Guidelines: Wildfires and Wildfire Smoke"},"note":"CDC states that wildfire smoke irritates eyes, nose, throat and lungs and advises reducing smoke exposure. This does not establish smoke composition, intensity, exposure, injury, diagnosis, or a particular person.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:macro-b1819-ash-eye-rinse","source_ref":"source:macro-b1819-cdc-wildfire-ash","anchor":{"section":"Protect yourself from ash"},"note":"CDC states that ash can irritate eyes and that ash reaching eyes should be washed off as soon as possible. This supports only a simple particulate-removal response, not an eye diagnosis, medication, treatment protocol, recovery, or availability of water.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:macro-b1819-chicken-nesting-egg-laying","source_ref":"source:macro-b1819-umd-home-chicken-flock","anchor":{"section":"Egg production/breeding; Housing"},"note":"University of Maryland Extension states that chickens kept for egg production need access to nests and that pullets should have an opportunity to find nests before laying. This supports a qualitative nest-and-laying relation, not a current hen, egg, nest, clutch, fertility, season, collection, or historical household stock.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:macro-b1819-storm-hung-wood-hazard","source_ref":"source:macro-b1819-usfs-storm-trees","anchor":{"section":"Winter Storm Impacts Huron Zone of the Forests"},"note":"US Forest Service warns that broken limbs can remain hung in canopies as overhead hazards and drop without warning after a storm. This establishes no local storm, tree, branch, fall time, safe route, work method, tool, or injury.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:macro-b1819-blood-feeding-flies-cattle-disturbance","source_ref":"source:macro-b1819-unl-horn-flies","anchor":{"section":"Horn Flies on Pastured Cattle; cattle defensive behaviors"},"note":"UNL Extension identifies horn flies as blood-feeding and reports that rising fly numbers prompt cattle defensive behaviors. This is a general species interaction; it does not identify a fly, animal, season, location, infestation, disease, treatment, or production result.","review_status":"approved"}
  ],
